00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.000
ISAAC HEPWORTH: Hi, I'm Isaac.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.210
In this video, I'm going to give
you an overview of Apigee's API

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.190
monitoring capabilities.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.430
APIs are the foundation of
modern connected applications.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.860
From mobile banking
to self-driving cars,

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.810
from retail to
health care, nearly

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:23.170
every digital interaction
today is underpinned by APIs.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.570
Therefore, API
service degradations

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.730
can directly impact your
customers' experience,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.880
your reputation, and
even your revenue.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.680
To ensure seamless
connected experiences

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.660
for developers,
customers, and partners,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.680
operations teams today want
to assure API service quality,

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.400
proactively identify API
issues before customers do,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.620
quickly diagnose
those issues, and take

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.783
appropriate remedial
actions fast.

00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:50.200
All of these capabilities are
available through the Apigee

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.540
Edge platform to empower
your operations teams

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.950
and increase API reliability.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.000
Let me show you.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.500
Apigee API monitoring
can alert you

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.080
to live issues via
Slack, PagerDuty,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.510
or in this case, email.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.240
Clicking through, you can see
the alert details, including

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.740
a link to a playbook
that recommends

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.900
remedial action for this
particular type of issue.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.380
Your playbook could be a wiki
page, a diagnostics manual,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.440
a triage flowchart, or
other relevant documentation

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.140
of your choice.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.390
Let's go a little deeper here.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.370
Clicking through,
you are now taken

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:24.310
to a rich, contextual view of
the error and the circumstances

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:25.090
surrounding it.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.880
In the first section, you
can find the basic condition

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.340
details, and again, a
link to the playbook.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.340
Scrolling down, you can see
these powerful co-occurrence

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.500
matrices, two-dimensional
views that

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.870
allow you to quickly visualize
and pinpoint the nature

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.160
and source of the error.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.010
Operations teams can often
spend hours navigating

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:43.990
multiple systems to
gather and cross-correlate

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.420
this information, but
Apigee API monitoring

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.880
can provide it for you
in just a few clicks.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.920
You can also click the
report icon on the right

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.360
to generate a custom report.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.730
You'll see it's already
pre-populated with just

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.770
the right set of filters
specific to this error

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.810
condition to help
others investigate

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.130
a similar issue faster.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.200
From here, click on the
menu button on the right

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.820
to navigate to
the timeline view.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.340
This gives the ability to view
the performance of components

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.823
of your API system over time.

00:02:07.823 --> 00:02:10.240
For this particular proxy,
we're looking at the past three

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:11.800
months of activity.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.560
Creating an alert trigger in
Apigee Edge is straightforward.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.350
In the monitoring dashboard,
click on Alerts, and then

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:18.580
the button to add an alert.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:19.960
You can specify the condition.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.390
You can set thresholds
or add dimensions.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.400
And under dimensions,
you can also

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:25.930
select a collection
of interests.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.510
A collection is a grouping
of proxies and targets

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.490
that you want to monitor
closely as a unit.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:32.500
Apigee API monitoring
lets you see

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.180
a snapshot of
historical condition

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:35.980
data overlaid with
your thresholds

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.050
to set just the right values.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.610
This is just a quick sample.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.160
To get these monitoring
capabilities for your traffic,

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.350
all you need to do is
to create an Apigee

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.590
Edge, a proxy for your APIs,
and direct client applications

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:48.370
to use it.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.920
And of course, all of
the data presented here

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.860
is available via our own
APIs, so you can connect it

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:56.890
to your analysis, audit, and
service management tools.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:00.510
OK.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.690
That's a quick introduction
to Apigee Edge API monitoring.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.960
It provides contextual
insights into API performance,

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.690
helps quickly diagnose
issues, and facilitates

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.120
remedial actions for service
assurance and business

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:12.960
continuity.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.990
If you want to know more, dive
into the product documentation

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.610
or visit the community.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:18.940
That's it from me.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.300
Until next time, keep
those APIs running.

